    "lawCode": "Amd §211-f, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4921\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to the takeover and\nrestructuring of struggling schools\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill will provide that any schools eligible for an appointment of\nan external receiver would be designated as \"struggling\" or \"persistent-\nly struggling\" schools instead of \"failing\" or \"persistently failing\"\nschools.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill would amend subpart H of part EE of Chapter 56\nof the Laws of 2015 in order to provide that those schools which would\nbe eligible for appointment of an external receiver shall be designated\nas \"struggling\" or \"persistently struggling\" schools, instead of the\ncurrent designation of \"failing\" or \"persistently failing.\"\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nChapter 56 of the Laws of 2015 designates schools that are eligible for\nappointment of an external receiver to be labeled as \"failing\" or\n\"persistently failing.\" However, this is an inappropriate and demeaning\nterm which is unfair to the affected communities, teachers and students.\n\nThese schools have suffered from years of chronic underperformance and\nunderfunding, which have resulted in these schools being placed in a\nnegative accountability status. Chapter 56 of the Laws of 2015 provides\nan opportunity for the current superintendent or Chancellor of these\nschools to obtain additional powers for a one or two year period to\nassist in turning these schools around, as well as $7.5 million in fund-\ning to assist schools that have been on the list of most underperforming\nschools over the last ten years.\n\nThese schools are located in our highest needs districts and serve a\nlarge number of students with disabilities and English language lear-\nners, as well as students living in poverty. The stigma of being labeled\nas a failing or persistently failing school only further perpetuates the\nissues and difficulties these schools face. By changing the designation\nstatus from failing and persistently failing to struggling and persis-\ntently struggling, this bill indicates that this is a temporary situ-\nation for these schools which can be addressed and overcome, and that\nadditional management powers and funding will be associated with such\nschools in order to improve their overall academic performance in order\nto be removed from their more negative accountability status.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill. Amends Chapter 56 of the Laws of 2015\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nImmediate\n\n",
